<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>KhaozEngine.Catalog</PackageId>
    <Version>$(KhaozEngineVersion)</Version>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <Description>Game-agnostic tunable-content catalog (in Foundation): the content type registry with its field schemas and the six engine content types, the row codecs, the content-addressed pack format (KECC chunks, KECM manifests, KECT text chunks, KECR remap rules), the kec/ domain-separated SHA-256 digests, the pack store seam with its file-system store and pack reader, the read-side snapshot, the remap rules, and the pure validator with its KEC finding codes. Integers everywhere, little endian through BinaryPrimitives, unsigned LEB128 varints, and decoders that return a stable reason token instead of throwing. Takes no third-party dependency at all, so a game client pulls the read side with no database in its graph.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../KhaozEngine.Primitives/KhaozEngine.Primitives.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- ContentTypeTable is the loaded runtime's STORAGE rather than its seam, so spec 9.1 declares it
         internal and a consumer reads through ContentRuntime. The facts about its shape (Offsets sized to
         the highest live id plus one, KeyIds open addressed with 0 empty, no Keys array) are exactly what
         the runtime tests pin, so the test assembly sees inside and nothing else does. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="KhaozEngine.Catalog.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
